{} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/329"} "Dietrich-Eckart-Freilichtbühne, Reichssportfeld, Berlin"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/70125"} "111 from 14 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Placement determined by best scores on apparatus in individual all-around event."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Czechoslovakia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/31163"} "Alois Hudec"] ", who was 1934 World Champion on rings, won the gold medal quite easily, with a margin of 0.566 points over Yugoslavian " [:a {:href "/athletes/31521"} "Leon Štukelj"] ". This was one of only two men’s gymnastics events in Berlin not won by German or Swiss gymnasts, the other being the " [:a {:href "/results/70124"} "horizontal bar"] ", won by Finland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/29846"} "Aleksanteri Saarvala"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Štukelj was Slovenian and, many years later, would become a Slovenian sports legend, after that country became independent in the 1990s. Berlin ended his distinguished Olympic career, as he had been " [:a {:href "/results/70062"} "individual all-around champion at Paris in 1924"] ". He competed at Paris and Amsterdam (1928), winning a total of six Olympic medals, with other golds in the " [:a {:href "/results/70069"} "1924 horizontal bar"] " and the " [:a {:href "/results/70086"} "1928 rings"] ". He also won bronze in " [:a {:href "/results/70074"} "1928 in the individual all-around"] "."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {} "Compulsory Exercise Points"] [:th {} "Optional Exercise Points"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/31163"} "Alois Hudec"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/TCH"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/TCH.png"}] "TCH"]] [:td {} "19.